 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation (perchè, si possente without its accent, Madame, and Ferrando's bracket before che alle vive faville). Ferrando and Guglielmo both sing per implorar pietade in flebil metro!, as the print has it; each has his own phrase. Dorabella's last line, Sorella, che facciamo?, runs on into No. 14 (Come scoglio), whose first bar holds -ciamo?: it belongs to this piece, which shows and speaks the line whole, but only Sorella che fac- is aligned here. Don Alfonso's first miei in bar 13 is sung in two syllables, and its IPA follows the score. IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L28, IPA checked against Castel). IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
